        <h2>Overview</h2>

        <p>
            A fierce, six-legged, cat-like beast from Cherokee and Appalachian folklore that stalks
            the woodlands of East Tennessee and Kentucky.
        </p>

        <h2>Appearance</h2>

        <p>
           Body Structure: A massive, cougar-like body featuring six powerful legs instead of four.
            It uses its rear four legs for running and its front two paws for slashing prey.
             Sharp, dagger-like fangs and piercing, glowing yellow eyes.
             Folklore states it occasionally stands on its hind legs, revealing a distorted, half-human posture. 
        </p>

        <h2>Habitat</h2>

        <p>
           It hides inside deep limestone caves, rocky mountain hollows, and abandoned coal mines during the day.
        </p>

        <h2>Reported sightings</h2>

        <ul>
           <li> A local resident named Mr Gaisser reported coming within ten feet of a six-foot-long, ferocious feline. He noted it had exceptionally long claws and could "attack and dispatch a man as easily as a hog."</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Theories</h2>

        <p>
           <ul>
            <li> Early European settlers adapted the story into a cautionary tale. They blamed the creature for missing livestock,
                 missing hunting hounds, and unsettling noises heard in the dead of night. </li>
           </ul>
        </p>

